.TH OOB-USB-SERIAL 1 "2026-08-31" "oob-usb-serial 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
oob-usb-serial \- out-of-band USB serial console multiplexer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oob-usb-serial
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oob-usb-serial
discovers every USB-to-serial adapter attached to the host and opens each one
as a named window inside a single detached
.BR tmux (1)
session, each window running
.BR tio (1)
on the adapter's stable
.I /dev/serial/by-id
path. Adapters are matched by their stable udev
.B ID_SERIAL
identifier rather than by their
.I /dev/ttyUSBx
node, which is not stable across reboots or replugs.
.PP
It is intended to turn a small Linux host, such as a Raspberry Pi Zero with a
USB hub and several serial adapters, into an out-of-band access box reachable
over SSH.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B discover
List discovered adapters as raw tab-separated records
(device node, ID_SERIAL, vendor, model, by-id path).
.TP
.B list
Human-friendly table of adapters and their resolved configuration.
.TP
.B start
Start the detached tmux session, one window per adapter running tio.
.TP
.B attach
Attach to the session, starting it first if it is not running.
.TP
.B stop
Terminate the session.
.TP
.B status
Report whether the session is running.
.TP
.B config
Interactively map each discovered adapter into
.IR devices.conf ,
prompting for a window name and serial line settings (adapters already mapped
are skipped). Writing usually requires root.
.TP
.B help
Show usage information.
.SH "TMUX KEYS"
Within the session:
.B Ctrl-b n
/
.B Ctrl-b p
switch consoles,
.B Ctrl-b w
lists them,
.B Ctrl-b d
detaches, and
.B Ctrl-b R
relaunches a console that has closed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/oob-usb-serial/oob-usb-serial.conf
Global defaults: baud, data, parity, stop, flow, session name, log directory
and OOB user.
.TP
.I /etc/oob-usb-serial/devices.conf
Per-device mappings in INI format. Each
.RI [ section ]
names a tmux window and contains a
.B match
glob (against udev ID_SERIAL) plus optional
.BR baud ,
.BR data ,
.BR parity ,
.B stop
and
.B flow
keys.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/oob-usb-serial
Bash tab-completion for the subcommands.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tio (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR udevadm (8)
